[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186059843 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a462c56c-120f-5159-b0b8-404f14801fe3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059399 :question/problem "What is the value of $\\cos 225^\\circ?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $225^\\circ$ corresponds to the point $\\left( -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\cos 225^\\circ  = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059400 :question/problem "What is the value of $\\sin \\left(\\dfrac {7\\pi} 4\\right)?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}$ corresponds to the point $\\left( \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin \\left(\\dfrac {7\\pi} 4\\right)  = -\\dfrac {\\sqrt 2} 2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059845 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d25800d2-aaa9-5069-8bc6-84b04968eef5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059401 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\csc 120^\\circ.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $120^\\circ$ corresponds to the point $\\left( -\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin 120^\\circ= \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\csc{\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\csc 120^\\circ = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin 120^\\circ } = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt 3} = \\dfrac{2\\sqrt 3}{3}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059402 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\tan \\left(\\dfrac {4 \\pi} 3\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ corresponds to the point $\\left( -\\dfrac{1}{2}, -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\cos\\left( \\dfrac {4\\pi} {3}\\right)  = -\\dfrac 1 2.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin\\left( \\dfrac {4\\pi} {3}\\right)  = -\\dfrac {\\sqrt 3} 2.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\tan{\\theta} = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\right)  = \\dfrac{\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\right) }}{\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\right) }} = \\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac {\\sqrt 3} 2\\right)}{\\left(-\\dfrac 1 2\\right)} = \\sqrt{3}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186233307 :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡{210}^{∘}=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${210}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{210}^{∘}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dcc95cae-2a41-5292-8333-6c71877ee9b8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dcc95cae-2a41-5292-8333-6c71877ee9b8" :answer-field/id #uuid "dcc95cae-2a41-5292-8333-6c71877ee9b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6f15e5df-c04e-5387-8967-9ef24d2b5564" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6f15e5df-c04e-5387-8967-9ef24d2b5564" "answer-94e173d4-a33d-579b-918a-c205ed8ceb06" "answer-e621f025-9b39-5453-a07b-7ce0adbcc585" "answer-b9878a3e-b0e9-50aa-b40c-a9ca56e85f59" "answer-c372e4ce-1412-5124-ab09-93c80b19bf23"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f15e5df-c04e-5387-8967-9ef24d2b5564" :answer/id #uuid "6f15e5df-c04e-5387-8967-9ef24d2b5564" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94e173d4-a33d-579b-918a-c205ed8ceb06" :answer/id #uuid "94e173d4-a33d-579b-918a-c205ed8ceb06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e621f025-9b39-5453-a07b-7ce0adbcc585" :answer/id #uuid "e621f025-9b39-5453-a07b-7ce0adbcc585" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9878a3e-b0e9-50aa-b40c-a9ca56e85f59" :answer/id #uuid "b9878a3e-b0e9-50aa-b40c-a9ca56e85f59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c372e4ce-1412-5124-ab09-93c80b19bf23" :answer/id #uuid "c372e4ce-1412-5124-ab09-93c80b19bf23" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a462c56c-120f-5159-b0b8-404f14801fe3" :question/id #uuid "a462c56c-120f-5159-b0b8-404f14801fe3" :question/math-academy-id "q-123595" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c4